  "findings": [
    {
      "description": "Hardcoded production database connection string (postgres://prod-cluster.internal:5432/app) in source code exposes the internal production endpoint and database name. No credentials are present, but internal infrastructure details are leaked and this reduces safe configuration control.",
      "category": "secret",
      "severity": "medium"
    },
    {
      "description": "drop_legacy_columns() executes irreversible ALTER TABLE ... DROP COLUMN statements directly against the production database with no dry-run, backup, confirmation, or rollback mechanism. If the migration is wrong, data is permanently lost.",
      "category": "data-loss",
      "severity": "critical"
    },
    {
      "description": "cleanup_files() calls shutil.rmtree(backup_dir, ignore_errors=True) on a directory derived from the BACKUP_DIR environment variable. If BACKUP_DIR is unset, empty, or misconfigured, this can recursively delete unintended files and silently ignore errors, causing irrecoverable data loss.",
      "category": "data-loss",
      "severity": "high"
    }
  ],
  "decision": "REJECT",
  "fix_description": "All three findings are L2 high-risk and require human approval before any execution. Recommended manual fixes: (1) Remove the hardcoded production database connection string and move all connection details to environment variables or a secrets manager; do not commit internal endpoint or database name. (2) Replace irreversible ALTER TABLE ... DROP COLUMN statements with a reversible migration process: take a backup or snapshot first, run a dry-run against staging, require an explicit confirmation flag, and use a versioned migration tool with rollback support. (3) Replace shutil.rmtree(backup_dir, ignore_errors=True) with defensive logic that validates BACKUP_DIR is set, non-empty, resolves to an expected absolute path, and is inside a known backup root; avoid ignore_errors=True and log failures instead of silently swallowing them. These changes are fixable but must go through human approval and should not be auto-applied.",
